.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii perso_linux.9
.\"
.TH PERSO_LINUX 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
perso_linux \- Linux i386 binary compatibility personality
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/exec/perso/personality.h>
.PP
.BI "extern struct personality " personality_linux ";"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B perso_linux
personality enables the execution of unmodified Linux i386 binaries on the Substrate kernel. It maps Linux system call numbers to the corresponding internal kernel implementations.
.PP
This personality is feature-rich and includes extensive tracing metadata to support debugging of foreign binaries.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS
.TP
.B Syscall Mapping
The personality maps standard Linux 2.6/3.x syscall numbers:
.RS
.IP * 2
.B __NR_exit (1)
.IP * 2
.B __NR_fork (2)
.IP * 2
.B __NR_read (3)
.IP * 2
.B __NR_clone (120)
.RE
.TP
.B Specialized Shims
Several system calls require specialized shim functions to adapt Linux structures to internal kernel structures:
.RS
.TP
.B sys_uname (122)
Populates a `struct new_utsname` with "Linux" as the system name to satisfy libc startup checks.
.TP
.B sys_set_thread_area (243)
Handles GDT entry allocation for Thread Local Storage (TLS) segment registers (GS), critical for `glibc` initialization.
.TP
.B sys_socketcall (102)
Multiplexes socket operations (not yet fully implemented).
.RE
.SH TRACING
The Linux personality structure populates `syscall_names` and `syscall_fmts`, allowing the kernel's built-in tracer to display:
.IP
`execve("/bin/ls", ["ls", NULL], [ENV...]) = 0`
.PP
Instead of raw numbers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR personality (9),
.BR perso_native (9)
